TTY(1)                           User Commands                          TTY(1)

NAME
       tty - print the file name of the terminal connected to standard input

SYNOPSIS
       tty [OPTION]...

DESCRIPTION
       Print the file name of the terminal connected to standard input.

       If standard input is not a terminal, print "not a tty" and exit with a
       status of 1.  The message goes to standard output, not standard error:
       it is the answer to the question asked, not a failure of the command.

OPTIONS
       -s, --silent, --quiet
              print nothing, only return an exit status

       --help display this help and exit

       --version
              output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS
       0      standard input is a terminal

       1      standard input is not a terminal

       2      an invalid option was given

       3      a write error occurred

       The status is distinct from the output on purpose.  A script that only
       wants to know whether it is interactive uses -s and tests the status;
       one that needs the device name captures the output.

EXAMPLES
       Decide whether the script is interactive:

              if tty -s; then
                      echo "running on a terminal"
              fi

       Find out which terminal, and act on it -- this is what startx(1) does
       to work out whether it was started from a virtual console:

              t=$(tty)
              if expr "$t" : '/dev/tty[0-9][0-9]*$' > /dev/null; then
                      echo "on virtual console ${t#/dev/tty}"
              fi

NOTES
       The name reported is the one the kernel records for the descriptor, so
       it reflects where standard input actually points -- after a redirection
       it names the redirected terminal, not the one the shell started on.

SEE ALSO
       expr(1), stty(1), who(1)
